            'Convective available potential energy (CAPE) and convective inhibition (CIN) are complementary quantities read from a vertical atmospheric sounding on a Skew-T log-P diagram. CAPE is the area of positive buoyancy above the level of free convection (LFC) — the fuel for thunderstorm updrafts. CIN is the area of negative buoyancy between the surface parcel and the LFC — the energy barrier that must be overcome before free convection can begin. A capping inversion (sometimes called a "lid" or "cap") is typically a warm layer 1–3 km above the surface created by subsiding air from an upper-level ridge or the edge of the Saharan air layer (in the US context, the "elevated mixed layer" from the Mexican Plateau). It creates CIN by placing warm air above cooler surface air, making any rising surface parcel cooler than the environment in that layer and thus negatively buoyant. In an uncapped environment with CAPE present, any small trigger (afternoon heating over a warm field, sea-breeze convergence, a small hill) will initiate convection immediately; many small, scattered showers and weak thunderstorms develop throughout the afternoon, consuming the moisture and instability in a disorganised fashion. With a moderate cap (CIN of 50–200 J/kg), these weak triggers fail to break through; the atmosphere continues to destabilise throughout the afternoon (surface heating adds more CAPE, moist advection increases moisture), and the cap acts as a pressure cooker. When a stronger trigger — a cold front, dryline, or mesoscale convective boundary — finally breaks through the cap, all the accumulated CAPE is released simultaneously, producing organised, intense, long-lived convection (supercells, squall lines) rather than scattered weak cells. This is why the Southern Plains of the US are globally notorious for severe weather: the cap from the Mexican Plateau regularly suppresses convection while allowing CAPE values of 3,000–6,000 J/kg to build before explosive severe weather outbreaks in spring. Option A confuses CIN with wind shear; CIN is measured in J/kg (an energy) and is a buoyancy integral, not a shear metric. Option C describes the cold pool from a storm\'s downdraft, which is a separate concept from CIN; while cold pool dynamics are important for storm maintenance and new storm triggering, they are not the definition of CIN. Option D is completely fabricated; CIN is not a measure of precipitation recycling.',
