            'Ensemble forecasting was developed directly from Lorenz\'s theory of atmospheric predictability. Since the atmosphere is a chaotic system, any NWP forecast represents only one of many possible future atmospheric evolutions consistent with the current observational uncertainty. A single deterministic model run gives a "best guess" initial state and integrates it forward — but this single trajectory may diverge wildly from reality if the initial state was in a particularly sensitive region of the atmospheric phase space. Ensemble systems (the ECMWF Ensemble Prediction System uses 50+1 members; the NCEP GEFS uses 30 members) instead sample the space of plausible initial conditions by perturbing the initial state in directions of maximum uncertainty (singular vectors, bred vectors, or ensemble Kalman filter perturbations). Each member then evolves independently forward in time. The results of the ensemble provide probabilistic information: (1) the ensemble mean tends to outperform any individual deterministic run at medium range because the averaging process cancels out unpredictable high-wavenumber (small-scale) variability while preserving the predictable planetary-scale signal; (2) the spread of the ensemble members quantifies forecast uncertainty — a tight cluster of members all showing similar weather evolution means high predictability; a widely dispersed ensemble means the atmosphere is in a chaotic, sensitive regime where forecasts are uncertain; (3) the frequency of different outcomes in the ensemble provides calibrated probabilistic forecasts (e.g., "70% chance of significant precipitation"). Option A is incorrect: ensemble members are not run to catch programming errors; they are run to sample initial condition uncertainty, and the members use the same model code. Option C is incorrect: ensemble members run on the same global domain, not on separate tiled regions; the parallel computing structure is an implementation detail, not the scientific rationale. Option D is partially true (multi-model ensembles do quantify model uncertainty by using different parameterisations), but the standard single-model ensemble (like ECMWF EPS or GEFS) perturbs initial conditions, not physics schemes; the primary rationale is initial condition uncertainty and chaos, not model parameterisation differences.',
